Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:05:47 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 24/52] VitaAutotests bump again and more small fixes (#4242)

* kernel: return from a ForVM suspend once the thread has stopped

sceKernelSuspendThreadForVM returned as soon as it asked the thread to
stop, so a context read right after it could race with the thread still
running guest code. A thread woken while suspended also went back to
its code and wrote its result before it stopped. On a Vita the suspend
only returns once the thread has stopped, and a woken thread reports
SUSPENDED and stays in its wait call until the resume, with the context
it had while it waited. Now the suspend waits for the thread to stop,
and a woken thread stays in its wait call as suspended. A wait whose
timeout passed while suspended leaves its queue first, so a later wake
can't take the place of the timeout as it can't on a Vita, and stops on
its way back to its code, which a context read waits for.

Found in the review of #4240
(see https://github.com/Vita3K/Vita3K/pull/4240#discussion_r4273124329).

* kernel: keep the ForVM and debugger suspends apart

The ForVM suspend shared suspend_requested with the debugger's, so a
ForVM resume let go of a thread that the debugger or
sceKernelWaitExceptionForMono had suspended, and the debugger's resume
let go of a thread the ForVM count held. Now the ForVM suspend only
uses its count, and the thread stays suspended while either holds it.
The debugger's resume returns whether the thread runs again, so the
gdb stub doesn't wait for a thread the ForVM count still holds.

Found in the review of #4240
(see https://github.com/Vita3K/Vita3K/pull/4240#discussion_r4273124415).

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

Move AUTOTESTS_REF to the latest VitaAutotests commit. It adds
thread_suspend_for_vm_stops, which checks that a ForVM suspend returns
once the thread has stopped, a case to thread_context_vfp for a thread
woken while suspended, a case to thread_status_matrix for a signal
after a suspended wait timed out, cases to msgpipe_vectors for long
vector lists, their total size and empty pieces, and
msgpipe_failed_sizes for the sizes failed message pipe calls write. The
first two pass, and msgpipe_vectors and msgpipe_failed_sizes still
fail, since Vita3K doesn't have the vector calls.

* kernel: fail a CB delay of 0 like a Vita

sceKernelDelayThreadCB(0) returned 0 and ran the pending callbacks. On
a Vita it fails with SCE_KERNEL_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, like
sceKernelDelayThread(0), and runs no callbacks.

* kernel: run a CB wait's pending callbacks only once its object is found

Every CB wait ran the pending callbacks first, so a wait on an unknown
UID ran them before it failed. On a Vita such a wait fails at once and
runs no callbacks, while one that finds its object runs them even when
it ends at once or has a timeout of 0. The callbacks now run right
before the wait, after the lookup and the checks of the export.

* kernel: take a free lightweight mutex without running callbacks

sceKernelLockLwMutexCB ran the pending callbacks even when it took a
lightweight mutex no other thread held. A Vita takes such a mutex in
user code, so it runs no callbacks. It now only runs them, and waits,
when another thread holds the mutex.

* kernel: report a size of 0 when a message pipe wait fails

A message pipe send or receive whose wait timed out, was canceled or
ended with a delete left its size untouched. On a Vita it writes the
size it moved, which is 0 here, since Vita3K only moves data once a
wait ends. A call that fails on its arguments still writes nothing.

* kernel: let a message pipe transfer move a list of pieces

MsgPipe::send and MsgPipe::receive took one host buffer. They now take
a list of pieces in guest memory and copy each one straight to or from
the pipe's buffer, so the vector calls won't need to copy their pieces
into a buffer of their own first. A plain call passes one piece.
Nothing else changes.

* kernel: implement the message pipe vector calls

sceKernelSendMsgPipeVector, sceKernelReceiveMsgPipeVector, their CB
forms and their try forms returned an unimplemented error. They now
check the guest's list of pieces and hand it to the pipe as it is,
which sends the pieces as one message and receives into them in order.
A list has no limit on its number of pieces, where a Vita takes at
least 8192. Like on a Vita, every piece needs a buffer even when it is
empty, only the size of all the pieces together is checked against the
pipe, and a list of only empty pieces ends at once in both modes,
unlike a plain ASAP call of 0 bytes.

(cherry picked from commit 41cac41ade11c639e2b692ee0699c5b8f786744d)
